So the implementation:

```swift
public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable {
    case malformedFormat
}

public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note {
    let parts = text.split(separator: "\n---\n", maxSplits: 3, omittingEmptySubsequences
: false)
    guard parts.count == 4 else {
        throw NoteParseError.malformedFormat
    }
    return Note(
        id: String(parts[0]),
        title: String(parts[1]),
        body: String(parts[3]),
        tags: extractTags(from: String(parts[3])),
        pinned: String(parts[2]) == "1"
    )
}
```

Note that the `Note` struct's fields don't have public init parameters — a struct
 with `let` properties inside the same module gets a memberwise initializer that's
 internal by default. Since `parseNote` is in the same file/module, the internal member
wise init is fine. But the parameter order of the memberwise init is: id, title, body
, tags, pinned.

